{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Regime shift bias: the model is broadly too bullish. After ~day 10–15 it maintains prices in the low-70s while realized prices trend down into the mid-60s; apply a negative level shift to the whole path when recent momentum turns down.",
    "Horizon-dependent error: near-term (days 1–7) errors are small-to-moderate, but medium/long horizon (days 10–30) errors become strongly positive. Use an increasing downward correction with horizon (e.g., subtract more as lead time increases).",
    "Failure to capture drawdowns: when the last 5–10 days show weakening/rollover, the model still extrapolates prior strength. Add a mean-reversion term toward a trailing average (e.g., 10–20d) and dampen trend extrapolation when recent returns turn negative.",
    "Overshoot after spikes: following a local spike/peak in history (around 71–73), the model forecasts continued elevated levels, but realized prices revert and then sell off. Cap post-spike forecasts and enforce faster reversion after large positive moves.",
    "Lag in turning points: the model reacts late to the transition from uptrend to downtrend (keeps predicting rising/flat while actual declines). Introduce a turning-point detector (e.g., short MA crossing below long MA) that flips the correction sign quickly.",
    "Volatility asymmetry: the model underestimates downside tail risk; realized paths show sharper drops than forecast. Add downside skew: when recent volatility rises, widen lower bands / apply extra negative adjustment."
  ]
}
